    'Antiphospholipid syndrome (clinical criteria such as recurrent early miscarriage, fetal death, early pre-eclampsia or thrombosis, with persistent lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin or anti-β2-glycoprotein I antibodies) is a treatable cause of recurrent miscarriage. Low-dose aspirin plus prophylactic LMWH from a positive pregnancy test improves live-birth rates. Warfarin is teratogenic (embryopathy at 6–12 weeks), and steroids do not improve outcomes and increase complications. Women with previous thrombosis need therapeutic-dose LMWH.',
