    'Acute uterine inversion causes profound shock (partly neurogenic from traction on the ligaments) and haemorrhage. It is risked by fundal placentation, cord traction without a contracted uterus and fundal pressure. The uterus should be replaced immediately (Johnson manoeuvre: pushing the fundus up through the cervix), with resuscitation; if the placenta is attached, it is usually left until replacement. Tocolytics or general anaesthesia may be needed to relax a cervical ring; hydrostatic (O\'Sullivan) or surgical methods are alternatives. Uterotonics are given only after replacement.',
