    'Transient tachypnoea of the newborn results from delayed clearance of fetal lung fluid, which normally begins with catecholamine surges and sodium channel activation during labour. It is commoner after elective caesarean section without labour, in term or near-term babies, and with maternal diabetes. It causes tachypnoea from soon after birth, mild oxygen need and X-ray signs of retained fluid (fissural fluid, perihilar streaking), resolving within 24–72 hours with supportive care. Surfactant-deficient RDS affects preterm babies and shows a ground-glass appearance with air bronchograms.',
