    'Early-onset neonatal sepsis (first 72 hours) is acquired from the maternal genital tract; group B streptococcus and E. coli are the main organisms, with Listeria less often. Risk factors include maternal GBS colonisation or bacteriuria, prolonged rupture of membranes, maternal fever and prematurity. Intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis (IV penicillin) reduces early-onset GBS disease. Empirical treatment is IV ampicillin or benzylpenicillin plus gentamicin after a blood culture. Ceftriaxone is avoided in neonates because it displaces bilirubin and precipitates with calcium.',
