    'Universal newborn hearing screening aims to detect permanent hearing loss early (screen by 1 month, diagnose by 3 months, intervene by 6 months) because language development depends on hearing in infancy. Otoacoustic emissions (sound produced by outer hair cells) are quick and non-invasive; automated auditory brainstem response is used for NICU babies and those failing OAE, because it also detects auditory neuropathy. Pure tone audiometry and tuning fork tests need a cooperative older child or adult.',
