    'VSD is the commonest congenital heart defect. Small (restrictive) defects produce a loud murmur because of the high pressure gradient but little shunt, and many (especially muscular and some perimembranous) close spontaneously in early childhood. Large VSDs cause heart failure at 4–8 weeks as pulmonary vascular resistance falls, failure to thrive and risk of pulmonary vascular disease, and need diuretics and surgical closure, usually in infancy. Endocarditis prevention focuses on good dental hygiene.',
